Data licensing for lexsync
==========================

The MIT License in LICENSE covers the lexsync *source code* only: the R package
under R_workflow/ and the Python package under python_workflow/. The lexical
corpora that lexsync bundles or fetches are NOT covered by it; they are
independent works with their own terms, summarised here and documented per
corpus, with full citations and retrieval dates, in 'corpora/ATTRIBUTION.md'.

Bundled derivatives
-------------------
The small example slices shipped inside the packages
('R_workflow/inst/extdata/' and 'python_workflow/src/lexsync/data/') and the
fuller derived corpora under 'corpora/derived/' are built from the 'wordfreq'
package by 'corpora/fetch_corpora.py', which is the only ingestion path that
produced them. Every row of every derived file records this in its 'source'
column. lexsync adds the orthographic-neighbourhood variables (Coltheart's N
and OLD20) and standardises the columns; the frequencies themselves are
wordfreq's.

wordfreq distributes its code and its data under different terms. The code is
under the Apache License 2.0, and the data files, which are what lexsync
derives from, are redistributable under

    Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

The bundled derivatives in this repository are therefore distributed under
CC BY-SA 4.0. You must (a) give appropriate credit to the original corpus
authors (see 'corpora/ATTRIBUTION.md'), (b) indicate if changes were made
(lexsync standardises columns and computes derived variables) and (c)
distribute any adaptations under the same licence.

Credit for SUBTLEX
------------------
wordfreq's frequencies are a blend of up to eight domains of text, among them
Wikipedia, OPUS OpenSubtitles, NewsCrawl, Google Books Ngrams, OSCAR, Twitter
and Reddit. The subtitles domain draws in part on the SUBTLEX word lists
SUBTLEX-US, SUBTLEX-UK, SUBTLEX-CH, SUBTLEX-DE and SUBTLEX-NL, created by Marc
Brysbaert and colleagues, which wordfreq carries by permission on two
conditions that pass through to anything derived from it:

  - work derived from wordfreq must credit the SUBTLEX authors, and
  - it must remain clear that SUBTLEX is freely available data.

Those citations are in 'corpora/ATTRIBUTION.md'. Note that SUBTLEX contributes
to the blend rather than constituting it: no lexsync corpus is a copy of a
SUBTLEX list, and the SUBTLEX resources listed in 'corpora/registry.yaml' are
separate corpora a user may load directly, not the source of anything bundled
here.

For Mandarin Chinese, wordfreq also imports a wordlist shipped with the Jieba
segmenter whose provenance its own documentation describes as unknown. Anyone
redistributing the bundled Chinese corpus, or relying on it for a claim about
frequency provenance, should read wordfreq's own account of its sources first.

Fetched corpora
---------------
Corpora retrieved on demand via the registry ('corpora/registry.yaml') are not
redistributed in this repository. Each carries its own licence and citation,
recorded in the registry and written to 'corpora/ATTRIBUTION.md' at fetch time.
Lexica derived through the optional 'wordfreq' connector carry the same terms
as the bundled derivatives above, since they come from the same source.

A note on wordfreq's licence history: wordfreq was released under the MIT
licence up to version 3.0.2 and relicensed to Apache 2.0 at 3.0.3. lexsync
requires 'wordfreq>=3.0,<4', which spans that change, so an installation may
carry either. The data terms above are unaffected.
